cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "ucgrb: Unit Commitment optimization for multi-area power systems using Gurobi Optimizer"
message: "If you use this software, please cite it using the metadata from this file."
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: "Yamaguchi"
    given-names: "Nobuyuki"
    name: "N. Yamaguchi"
    website: "https://www.yamaguchilab.info/%E3%83%A1%E3%83%B3%E3%83%90%E3%83%BC/%E6%95%99%E6%8E%88/"
  - family-names: "Manabe"
    given-names: "Yusuke"
    name: "Y. Manabe"
    website: "https://manabelab.com/#representative-partner"
repository-code: "https://github.com/YamaLabTUS/ucgrb"
abstract: "A Python package for unit commitment (UC) optimization of multi-area power systems connected by tie lines using Gurobi Optimizer. This tool can perform day-ahead scheduling and intra-day scheduling in succession to determine the UC for the target period (delivery date) for power systems in multiple areas connected by tie lines."
keywords:
  - "unit commitment"
  - "power system optimization"
  - "mixed-integer linear programming"
  - "gurobi optimizer"
  - "multi-area power systems"
  - "renewable energy"
  - "energy storage systems"
  - "tie lines"
license: MIT
version: "7.0.0"
date-released: 2026-09-02
references:
  - type: conference
    title: "Evaluation of PV Output Prediction Errors Due to Snow Coverage on Unit Commitment Using a Time-Varying Mean Reversion Model"
    authors:
      - family-names: "Komuro"
        given-names: "Takuto"
        name: "T. Komuro"
      - family-names: "Yamaguchi"
        given-names: "Nobuyuki"
        name: "N. Yamaguchi"
      - family-names: "Manabe"
        given-names: "Yusuke"
        name: "Y. Manabe"
      - family-names: "Ohtake"
        given-names: "Hideaki"
        name: "H. Ohtake"
    conference: "2023 IEEE PES Innovative Smart Grid Technologies - Asia (ISGT Asia)"
    year: 2023
    doi: "10.1109/ISGTAsia54891.2023.10372715"
    url: "https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/10372715"
    pages: "1-5"
    abstract: "In order to realize a carbon-neutral society, the large-scale introduction of renewable energy power generation into electric power systems is underway. Predicting photovoltaic (PV) output in a snowy area is extremely difficult because it is necessary to consider the amount of solar radiation and the amount of snow accumulated on the PV panels. As a result, it may fail to predict a decrease in PV output due to snow coverage, and the power plant operation schedule for the intra-day may have to be changed significantly from the day-ahead schedule. In this study, the impact of the decrease in the output of PV generation panels due to snow cover on the supply cost is evaluated by Monte Carlo simulation, in which a time-varying mean regression process model represents the uncertainty of the output of wind power generation. The target of the evaluation is a typical winter day in Japan's nine-tied control area. The calculation results are evaluated mainly in the Tohoku area, a snowy area where a large amount of wind power generation is expected to be introduced."
  - type: conference
    title: "Electricity Exchange between Control Areas Due to Tie Line Expansion and Demand Shifting Using Unit Commitment in Japan"
    authors:
      - family-names: "Okawara"
        given-names: "Shoki"
        name: "S. Okawara"
      - family-names: "Manabe"
        given-names: "Yusuke"
        name: "Y. Manabe"
      - family-names: "Yamaguchi"
        given-names: "Nobuyuki"
        name: "N. Yamaguchi"
    conference: "2023 IEEE PES Innovative Smart Grid Technologies - Asia (ISGT Asia)"
    year: 2023
    doi: "10.1109/ISGTAsia54891.2023.10372800"
    url: "https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/10372800"
    pages: "1-5"
    abstract: "Amid the growing concerns over global warming, Japan has set a goal to become carbon neutral by 2050. According to the long-term policy for a wide-area grid in the large scale introduction of renewable energy (RE) announced by the organization for Cross-regional Coordination of Transmission Operators (OCCTO) in March 2023, the construction cost is expected to be approximately 7 trillion JPY, half of which is for expanding tie lines between Hokkaido and Tohoku areas and between Tohoku and Tokyo areas using high-voltage direct-current submarine cables. Power demand shifting is one of the alternatives to the costly and time-consuming expansion of tie lines. In this study, the unit commitment simulation analysis is performed using the Japanese power system in 2050 for the electricity demand shift and expansion of tie lines, which have similar operational costs and RE output curtailment. The results of the analysis indicate that in the case of electricity demand shift, the amount of RE output curtailment decreases the most in Hokkaido area, where demand growth is small. Meanwhile, in the expansion of tie lines, the utilization between Tokyo and Chubu areas, where no expansion was implemented, increased."
  - type: conference
    title: "Analysis of Hydrogen Storage for Seasonal Leveling of Renewable Energy Generation in Scenarios of Japan's Generation Mix"
    authors:
      - family-names: "Oishi"
        given-names: "Yuna"
        name: "Y. Oishi"
      - family-names: "Okawara"
        given-names: "Shoki"
        name: "S. Okawara"
      - family-names: "Komuro"
        given-names: "Takuto"
        name: "T. Komuro"
      - family-names: "Manabe"
        given-names: "Yusuke"
        name: "Y. Manabe"
      - family-names: "Yamaguchi"
        given-names: "Nobuyuki"
        name: "N. Yamaguchi"
    conference: "2024 IEEE PES Innovative Smart Grid Technologies Europe (ISGT EUROPE)"
    year: 2024
    doi: "10.1109/ISGTEUROPE62998.2024.10863009"
    url: "https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/10863009"
    pages: "1-5"
    abstract: "The power sector is being decarbonized to achieve carbon neutrality in 2050, and the Group of Seven (G7) has decided to phase out coal-fired power generation by 2035. In this study, we set up coal phase-out scenarios for 2030, 2040, and 2050 and conducted a simulation analysis by unit commitment with an expanded introduction of renewable energy. In this study, we estimate the optimal capacity of hydrogen storage system (HSS) for various future supply scenarios in Japan through an inter-seasonal energy supply-demand leveling simulation. The proposed method is a time series simulation of unit commitment (UC) and inter-seasonal hydropower storage systems using mixed integer linear programming (MILP). As a result, the HSS significantly contributed to reducing the amount of Variable Renewable Energy (VRE) output suppression and effectively utilizing VRE in all future scenarios."
